<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions:</b></h2>
<h3><b>Q 1. What are common mistakes to avoid when laying pavers?</b></h3>
<p>Common mistakes when laying pavers include skipping proper excavation, using an insufficient gravel base, and failing to compact each layer correctly. Other issues include poor drainage planning, not using edge restraints, laying pavers on uneven sand, and ignoring joint filling. These errors often lead to sinking, shifting, weed growth, and uneven surfaces over time.</p>
<h3><b>Q 2. Does the paver base need to be perfectly level?</b></h3>
<p>No, the paver base should not be perfectly level, but it must be <b>smooth, evenly compacted, and properly graded</b>. A slight slope (usually about 1–2%) is required to allow water drainage away from structures. The key is consistency and stability rather than a perfectly flat surface.</p>
<h3><b>Q 3. Do I need gravel under a paver base?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, gravel is essential under a paver base. A compacted layer of crushed stone or gravel provides structural support, drainage, and load distribution. Without it, pavers are likely to shift, sink, or become uneven due to soil movement and water retention.</p>
<h3><b>Q 4. Can I lay pavers directly on dirt?</b></h3>
<p>No, laying pavers directly on dirt is not recommended. Soil alone cannot provide the stability or drainage needed for a long-lasting surface. Over time, the pavers will shift, sink, and become uneven. A proper base of compacted gravel and sand is required for durability and performance.</p>

<h2><b>Conclusion:</b></h2>
<p>A successful paver patio in Denver ultimately comes down to one critical factor: <b>proper base depth and preparation</b>. In Colorado’s freeze–thaw climate, the strength of the patio is not just in the pavers themselves, but in the layers beneath them. A well-compacted gravel base, correct excavation depth, and properly leveled bedding layer ensure the patio remains stable, prevents shifting, and resists cracking over time. Without this foundation, even high-quality pavers can fail prematurely, leading to uneven surfaces and costly repairs.</p>
<p>By focusing on correct installation techniques, choosing the right materials, and following a structured maintenance routine, homeowners can enjoy a patio that stays strong, functional, and visually appealing for decades. Proper planning and expert craftsmanship make all the difference in long-term performance, especially in a region like Denver where weather conditions constantly test outdoor structures.</p>
